# Normalise the line endings of the source files.
#
# The blog is built on Windows (locally) and on Linux (Cloudflare
# Pages). A file whose line endings differ between the two platforms
# produces different output:
#
#   - styled-jsx hashes the *text* of a `<style jsx>` block to build
#     its scope class name (`jsx-<hash>`). CRLF and LF produce
#     different hashes, so the same source file yields a different
#     class name on each platform.
#   - `src/components/layout/Footer.tsx` was the only file still
#     checked out with CRLF, so it was the only one whose scope hash
#     differed.
#
# `text=auto` lets git normalise the line endings in the repository,
# and `eol=lf` makes every platform check the files out with LF.
# That keeps the build output identical everywhere.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets must never be touched.
*.woff2 binary
*.woff  binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.webmanifest text eol=lf

# The shell scripts and the CI workflow must keep LF endings: Git
# Bash on Windows would otherwise check them out with CRLF, and the
# Linux Pages build would fail with `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M`.
*.sh text eol=lf
.github/workflows/*.yml text eol=lf
rust/rust-toolchain.toml text eol=lf
rust/tailwind.config.js text eol=lf
rust/wrangler.toml text eol=lf
